 Dealing cut-offs are usually set in the prospectus and applied consistently across the fund. They are not typically split by cash versus in-kind subscriptions and redemptions.\n- **Currency-hedged classes:** Hedging is generally done at class level, with hedging gains, losses, and costs attributed only to the hedged class. Exposure is typically kept within a set tolerance of that class's NAV, often 95–105%, though the exact limits depend on the Central Bank's guidance and the prospectus.\n\nTo answer this accurately, check:\n1. The current consolidated text of the Irish UCITS Regulations (S.I. 352/2011, as amended), specifically Regulation 26.\n2. The Central Bank of Ireland's UCITS Q&A, to confirm whether item 1030 exists and what it says.\n3. Any 2026 Central Bank notices or amendments.\n4. The fund's prospectus and supplement for its dealing deadlines.\n\nI can help you draft a comparison table once you've pulled those sources.","ttft_ms":3010,"type":"result","duration_ms":5081,"uuid":"098ea752-1662-4a81-bfe7-e5b2add920ad","ttft_stream_ms":411,"time_to_request_ms":59,"first_content_frame_ms":412,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
